3.text:
Under homoskedasticity and for a just identified model the Nagar
first-order approximation for the 2SLS bias can be written in the
conventional ``bias relative to OLS'' form

\begin{equation*}
{\rm Bias}_{2SLS}\simeq 
\frac{\sigma_{u v}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\;\frac{1}{\lambda},
\qquad 
\lambda=n\,\frac{\pi^{2}{\rm Var}(z)}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual first stage

\begin{equation*}
F=\frac{\hat\pi^{2}\sum z_{i}^{2}}{\hat\sigma_{v}^{2}}\;\approx\;
\lambda,
\end{equation*}
the bias of 2SLS relative to the OLS bias is

\begin{equation*}
R\;=\;\frac{{\rm Bias}_{2SLS}}{{\rm Bias}_{OLS}}
\;\approx\; \frac{1}{\lambda}\;\approx\;\frac{1}{F}\;, 
\end{equation*}
up to the $O_{p}(n^{-1})$ terms.  In particular
$E[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta]\approx(\sigma_{uv}/\sigma_{v}^{2})F^{-1}$,
i.e. $R\approx F^{-1}$ for the point Nagar approximation.

Hence on the point Nagar approximation

\begin{equation*}
R<0.10\iff F>10
\end{equation*}
is the familiar $F>10$ rule of thumb.  If the sampling variability of
$F$ is taken into account the Stock–Yogo maximal–relative–bias
critical value is the $5\%$ one-sided value of the Nagar distribution.
With the usual $F\approx\chi^{2}_{1}(\lambda)$ weak-instrument
approximation

$R_{up}\approx F^{-1}+z_{0.95}\sqrt{2}\,F^{-3/2}<0.10$
gives

\begin{equation*}
0.10\,F^{3/2}-F^{1/2}-z_{0.95}\sqrt{2}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $F^{1/2}\approx3.9\!-\!4.4$.  The conventional Stock–Yogo
$10\%$ maximal relative bias critical value is

\begin{equation*}
F_{10\%}^{\,SY}=16.38\approx16,
\end{equation*}
which is the $95\%$–confidence, worst case, value usually quoted.
In a strictly one-sided $95\%$ lower confidence bound for $\lambda$,
$\lambda_{L}=F-1.645\sqrt{2F}$, $\lambda_{L}=10$ gives
$F-1.645\sqrt{2F}=10$, i.e. $F\approx2.0\!-\!2.1\times10^{1}$, about
$F\approx 20\!-\!22$, often quoted as $F\approx21$, which collapses to
the point Nagar rule $F>10$ $R\approx1/F$.  This is the $F>10$
rule of thumb, the Stock–Yogo $10\%$ maximal relative-bias value being
$F\approx 16$–$17$.  The $F>10$ point approximation is the one that is
normally quoted in applied work.

2.text:
For a just identified model the 2SLS–OLS comparison can be written in the
Nagar form

\begin{equation*}
{\rm E}\!\left[\,\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta\,\right]\approx
\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma^{2}_{v}}\;\frac{1}{\mu^{2}},\qquad
\mu^{2}=n\pi^{2}\sigma_{z}^{-2}\sigma_{v}^{-2},
\end{equation*}

so that up to the sign

\begin{equation*}
{\rm RAB}\equiv 
\frac{{\rm bias}[\hat\beta_{2SLS}]}{{\rm bias}[\hat\beta_{OLS}]}
\approx \frac{K}{\mu^{2}}\approx\frac{1}{F-1}\approx\frac1F ,
\qquad K=1,
\end{equation*}

the usual $1/F$ form.  With $Z$ standardised

\begin{equation*}
t_{1}\equiv\sqrt{F}\;\xrightarrow{d}\;N\!\left(\delta,1\right),\qquad 
\delta\equiv\sqrt{\mu^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}

and in large samples $F\approx \delta^{2}+1$.  Hence a one-sided
$95\%$ lower confidence bound for the non-centrality is

\begin{equation*}
\delta_{L}=t_{obs}-z_{0.95}= \sqrt{F_{obs}}-1.645 .
\end{equation*}

Putting ${\rm RAB}\approx1/\mu^{2}=1/\delta^{2}$ the $10\%$ bias requirement
is $\delta_{L}^{2}>10$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{F_{obs}}-1.645>\sqrt{10}\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
\sqrt{F_{obs}}>\sqrt{10}+1.645\approx4.81,
\end{equation*}

i.e.

\begin{equation*}
F_{obs}^{*}\approx\bigl(\sqrt{10}+1.645\bigr)^{2}\approx 23\!-\!24
\end{equation*}

in the conventional $1/F$ Nagar form.  With the usual small-sample
Nagar form ${\rm RAB}\approx1/(F-2)$ this is $F_{true}>12$, whence
$\delta_{L}^{2}+1>12$,

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{F_{obs}}> \sqrt{11}+1.645\approx4.96, \qquad 
F_{obs}^{*}\approx 24.6\approx25,
\end{equation*}

the Stock–Yogo  $10\%$ maximal relative-bias critical value, often quoted
as $F\simeq23.1$ in the $1/F$ approximation.  This is the familiar
$F\gg10$ rule: $F\approx 10$ gives $ \approx10\%$ median bias, and about
$F\approx23$--$25$ is needed to be $95\%$ confident that the Nagar
relative bias is below $10\%$.  The $F-2$ small-sample correction is the
$ \approx 25$ value commonly quoted in the literature.

